  ],
  "text": "It is not lying to you. It has no concept of true. Only of plausible. You assume it is looking things up. Almost none of it works that way. It is predicting what text usually follows the text you gave it. And an invented answer that reads exactly right is a very good prediction. Which is why fabricated citations are so convincing. A real one has an author, a year, a journal and a plausible title. So does a fake one. There is nothing anywhere in the process that checks whether the paper exists. Giving it a search tool helps, because then there is something real to copy from. But the useful habit is simpler. Be most suspicious when it sounds most authoritative. Treat it as a confident first draft. Confidence is the one thing it always has. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
